<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karashahr</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yanqi, Kara-Shahr, Agni, Karasahr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karashahr, called Yanqi in Chinese sources, is an oasis town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In antiquity it was the centre of a Tocharian-speaking Buddhist city state on the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> and Kucha. The state paid tribute at times to Chinese dynasties and to steppe powers such as the Rouran and the Turks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karashahr.jpg" alt="Karashahr" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rolf Müller · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosque_yanqi_xinjiang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
